;; FH-INPUT-003 — the controlled round trip as a user-visible fact.
;;
;; Caret position, IME composition and dropped characters are DOM
;; behaviours. A structural assertion cannot see one — an element tree
;; can be perfectly shaped around a value the browser has already
;; restored out from under the user. So every row here is read back off a
;; live node in a real browser, after real events.
;;
;; The control row is what makes the rest non-vacuous. It is the SAME
;; field, wired through a handler one fact outside the door, typed at
;; exactly the same way; the browser drops characters from it. If the
;; control ever stops dropping, the suite says so rather than reporting a
;; confident green over a hazard that has quietly moved.
{:fh/id "FH-INPUT-003"
 :fh/law
 "In a real browser the controlled round trip holds as a user-visible fact: rapid typing yields a final value equal to the typed string character for character, the caret and a range selection survive the round trip unmoved, an IME composition completes intact, and none of it degrades while sibling cells on the same frame are dirty."

 :frame :fh-input/browser
 :query [:fh-input/text]
 :event :fh-input/typed
 :sibling-count 12

 :rapid
 {:note  "a fast typist outruns a batched round trip; the door does not"
  :seed  ""
  :typed "the quick brown fox"}

 :caret
 {:note      "a character inserted mid-string leaves the caret after it"
  :seed      "hello world"
  :at        5
  :ch        "X"
  :expected  "helloX world"
  :expected-caret 6}

 :selection
 {:note           "typing over a range replaces it and collapses the caret after the insert"
  :seed           "hello world"
  :from           0
  :to             5
  :ch             "J"
  :expected       "J world"
  :expected-caret 1}

 :composition
 {:note   "an IME builds one character over several composition updates"
  :seed   ""
  :steps  ["に" "にほ" "にほん"]
  :final  "にほん"}}
